---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Measured from "../../../components/Measured.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="methods"
  title="Choosing a method"
  description="Which of Deptheos's three stacking methods, Pyramid, Depth or Wavelet, to use for a stack, with the three compared on the same places at 100%."
  hasLabs
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Choosing a method</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Deptheos has three stacking methods: Pyramid, Depth and Wavelet. Each does best on some subjects and
      worst on others. The table is the short answer; the figure under it shows the three on the same places.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="table">
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="choose">
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Pyramid</th><th>Depth</th><th>Wavelet</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Hairs and fine detail over a far background</th><td class="best">Crisp</td><td class="weak">Soft</td><td class="best">Crisp</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Plain, out-of-focus backgrounds</th><td class="weak">Grainy at low Smoothness</td><td class="best">Smooth</td><td class="weak">Grainy, blotchy</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Something bright against something dark</th><td class="weak">Some glow</td><td class="best">Clean</td><td class="weak">Can streak</td></tr>
          <tr><th>A jump in depth, one part in front of another</th><td class="best">Crisp</td><td class="weak">Soft along the step</td><td class="weak">Can streak</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Colors</th><td>Blended from several frames</td><td>True to the source frames</td><td>Each pixel from one frame</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Frames out of focus order</th><td>Fine</td><td class="weak">Needs them in order</td><td>Fine</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Passes over the frames (I/O)</th><td>Once</td><td>Twice</td><td>Twice</td></tr>
          <tr>
            <th>
              Time for <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.stack.frames" /> JPEG frames of
              <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.stack.megapixels" digits={0} /> MP on an
              <Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.label" />, aligned with ECC
            </th>
            <td><Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.runs.pyramid.ecc.seconds" digits={1} /> s</td>
            <td><Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.runs.depth.ecc.seconds" digits={1} /> s</td>
            <td><Measured path="benchmarks.m5-max-pcb1-jpeg.runs.wavelet.ecc.seconds" digits={1} /> s</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Start with <strong>Pyramid</strong>, the default. Try <strong>Depth</strong> when a plain
        background comes out grainy or a dark area beside something bright comes out hazy. Reach for
        <strong>Wavelet</strong> when you want each pixel's color taken whole from a single frame rather than
        blended across frames (or if you're used to <code>focus-stack</code> and microscopy workflows). Where one
        method does better in one part of the picture and another elsewhere, stack with both and
        <a href="/docs/retouching/">paint</a> from the better render over the other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="compare">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The three on the same places</h2>
      <p>
        Results the app made from the hornet, spider and circuit-board sample stacks at full resolution, at
        100%, each method at its default settings. Choose a place, then flick between the methods.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure
      class="lab"
      id="methods-figure"
      data-lab="stops"
      data-assets="methods"
      data-stops="pyramid=Pyramid|depth=Depth|wavelet=Wavelet|wavelet-c2=Wavelet, filters on"
      data-stops-label="Method"
      data-start="pyramid"
      data-places="hairs=Hairs|green=Plain background|leg=Spider's leg|antenna=Antenna tip|board=Circuit board"
      data-places-label="Place"
      data-panels="{stop}-{place}.jpg=The result"
      data-center="384,256|384,256|300,256|384,256|384,256"
      data-why-by="place"
    >
      <div class="lab-body">
        <div class="pan"><img src="/media/labs/methods/pyramid-hairs.jpg" width="768" height="512" alt="Hairs on the hornet's head against the green, stacked with the pyramid method" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Five places, each stacked with every method. <em>Wavelet, filters on</em> is the wavelet with its
        consistency filters, which only the <a href="/docs/cli/">command line</a> can turn on
        (<code>--consistency 2</code>).
      </figcaption>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="hairs">
        <p>
          <strong>Hairs: the pyramid or the wavelet.</strong> Both choose separately for each size of detail,
          and their hairs are crisp. Depth's are softer, with a haze between them: each hair is thinner
          than the patch the map decides its depth over, so it is taken partly from frames where it is blurred.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="green" hidden>
        <p>
          <strong>A plain background: Depth.</strong> Nothing here is sharp in any frame. Depth
          gives the whole area one depth, so it comes from one or two frames and is smooth. The pyramid lets the
          sharpest frame at each spot dominate, and in a plain area that is whichever frame's noise scores
          highest, so it is grainy; the wavelet switches frames from pixel to pixel and is grainy too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="leg" hidden>
        <p>
          <strong>Bright against dark: Depth.</strong> In the pyramid the dark background beside the
          spider's pale leg is lifted to a haze: the leg's out-of-focus glow, let in through the coarse levels.
          Depth takes the background from frames where the leg is not spread over it, and it stays
          dark. The wavelet is blotchy there, with a fringe of the leg's color along its edge.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="antenna" hidden>
        <p>
          <strong>The antenna's tip: Pyramid or Depth.</strong> Both keep it crisp. In the wavelet
          the lower half of the tip is washed pale, with or without its filters. It gives each pixel the color
          of the frame whose brightness is closest to its gray picture, and that need not be a frame where the
          tip is sharp.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="board" hidden>
        <p>
          <strong>A circuit board: the pyramid.</strong> The bright metal sits at a jump in depth, beside a
          capacitor's tall black body. Depth softens it along the capacitor's edge, where its map runs
          from one depth to the other; the wavelet streaks gray into the black beside the bright metal, where
          neighboring pixels take their color from different frames.
        </p>
      </div>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="set-up">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How each works and its settings</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/docs/pyramid/">Pyramid</a>:</strong> splits each frame into levels of detail and
          blends them separately, giving the most weight to the sharpest. One setting: <em>Smoothness</em>, from
          crisp and noisier to calm and softer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/docs/depth-map/">Depth</a>:</strong> picks one depth per spot and takes each spot
          from the frame at that depth (which is why frames must be in focus order: blending frames 10 and 30
          lands on frame 20). Settings: <em>Smoothing</em>, <em>Radius</em>, and under Advanced,
          <em>Blend Range</em>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/docs/wavelet/">Wavelet</a>:</strong> merges the strongest detail at every scale into
          a sharp gray image, then gives each pixel the color of the frame whose brightness matches best. One
          setting: <em>Denoise</em>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
